WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.069
And the happiest people on Earth are officially...

00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:05.620
the Norwegians.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.300
They eat lots of fresh seafood.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.240
They live with breathtaking scenery.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.320
And the long dark winters don't seem to dampen
their spirits.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.170
For the first time, Norway's taken the top
spot on the United Nations World Happiness

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:19.170
Report.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.530
"Is Norway number one?

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:21.530
Wow!"

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:29.860
"It is not the weather because it's very cold,
but I think it's the nature and the people."

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:34.910
"It's a free country, you can speak your mind."

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.280
"We can appreciate changes and we are also
content with it."

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.079
Joar Vittersø is a psychologist and happiness
researcher.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:50.000
He's been trying to find out what makes people
contented with their lives - or not - since

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.150
the 1980s.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.699
And why are his own countrymen at the top
of the international ranking?

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:01.179
"People are more equal here than they are
in other places in the world.

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:06.250
And that has a lot of benefits to it, for
example people trust each other more when

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.500
they are on equal footing.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:14.780
And trust, we know, is a very important factor
for you to thrive and be comfortable and satisfied

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.940
with your life."

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.330
The northern city of Tromsø can serve as
an example.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.280
The educational system is excellent.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.790
The state provides health care, and unemployment
and poverty are low.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:35.560
Joar Vittersø works at the Arctic University
of Tromsø - the world's northernmost.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.070
He asserts that the positive attitude of people
in the high north plays a big part in their

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.450
overall contentment.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:48.180
"In some parts of the world you actually see
that the less daylight, the more depression

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.180
you can observe.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.620
That is not the case in Norway.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:58.480
In Northern Norway, it seems that people have
found ways of adapting to the dark period.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:05.490
They have activities they look forward to
conduct during the winter which compensate

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.950
and even more than compensate for the dark
period."

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.680
Skiing, the national sport, was even invented
in Norway.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.340
Tromsø has plenty of snow on the ground for
six months a year.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.170
The northern lights and sled-dog runs are
part of life.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.650
During the short summers, many Norwegians
love to go hiking with family and friends.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:32.370
The law allows everyone to make camp and even
build a fire in the open.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.019
"In Norwegian it's called 'allemannsretten'.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:38.261
That's a very long tradition in Norway.

00:02:38.261 --> 00:02:40.569
I think it's almost a thousand years old.

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:46.099
You are allowed to trespass other people's
property, you can even hunt there, but it

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:51.510
should be in agreement with the owner, but
the owner cannot deny you accessing it and

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.150
using it."

00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:00.959
From the age of two, children at this day-care
center in Tromsø spend their days outside

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:06.310
- no matter what the weather.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:15.230
Kjell Kampevoll co- founded this open-air
day-care center - one of many in Norway.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:20.629
He believes the key to happiness can be found
in the Norwegians' closeness to nature.

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:28.140
"Yes, I really think that my kids are really
happy.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.900
More kids play together without fighting."

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:40.260
Birgit Hem and Conrad Helgeland live with
their four children not far from Tromsø.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.269
They both have jobs and share the housework.

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:48.180
They make an extra effort to make their home
cozy - 'koselig' in Norwegian - especially

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.010
when it's cold outside.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:58.090
"I think Norwegians like having a cozy home
and candles when it's dark and make nice food

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.870
and be with their family and have a good time."

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:09.469
Birgit Hem is also quite happy that she doesn't
have to worry about her children's future.

00:04:09.469 --> 00:04:18.370
Joar Vittersø says governments can do much
to enhance the people's happiness - but individuals

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.370
can, too.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:26.000
"I think that we need more attention towards
what really matters in our lives.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.009
I think actually it's good for people to reflect
a little bit upon what is important for me,

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:38.740
what should I do with my life and not just
go along the flow and follow what is supposed

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.690
to be the road to success."

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:47.069
Norwegians have their own word for a special
kind of happiness.

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:50.910
'Utepils' - to sit in the sunshine and enjoy
a beer.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.699
During the Arctic summers with over 20 hours
of daylight, Norwegians have plenty of opportunity

00:04:55.699 --> 00:04:58.169
to experience these happy moments.

